        'rule.r3': 'The digit-sum rule &mdash; add every digit and the total must be divisible by 3.',
        'rule.r4': 'The last-two-digits rule &mdash; the number formed by the tens and ones digits must be a multiple of 4.',
        'rule.r7': 'The classical weighted rule for 7 &mdash; multiply the digits by 1, 3, 2, 6, 4, 5 from the right, repeating the cycle.',
        'rule.r8': 'The last-three-digits rule &mdash; the number formed by the last three digits must be a multiple of 8.',
        'rule.r9': 'The digit-sum rule again &mdash; the digits must add up to a multiple of 9.',
        'rule.r11': 'The alternating-sum rule &mdash; add and subtract the digits in turn, because 10 behaves as &minus;1.',
        'rule.r12': 'Divisible by 3 and by 4 at once &mdash; one weight row does the work of both.',
        'rule.r20': 'Ends in 00, 20, 40, 60 or 80 &mdash; only the last two digits matter.',
        'rule.r27': 'Split the digits into blocks of three from the right and add the blocks, because 1000 leaves remainder 1.',
        'rule.r45': 'Divisible by 9 and by 5 &mdash; the digit sum is a multiple of 9 and the last digit is 0 or 5.',
        'rule.note': 'Divisors made only of 2s and 5s collapse their weights to 0 — that is why only the last few digits matter for them. Divisors sharing no factor with 10 cycle forever. Mixed divisors like 12 and 45 do a little of each. One mechanism, three behaviors.',

        'proof.kicker': 'The Proof',
        'proof.h2': 'Three lines, no hand-waving',
        'proof.intro': 'This is the standard proof of <a href="#prior-art">Pascal’s criterion</a> — the same induction found in any elementary number theory treatment — written out in the vocabulary of this page.',
        'proof.s1': 'Subtracting <code>d</code> never changes a number’s remainder by <code>d</code>. So by induction, every weight has the same remainder as the power of ten it replaced: <code>weight(t) &equiv; 10<sup>t</sup> (mod d)</code>.',
        'proof.s2': 'Therefore the weighted digit sum is congruent to <code>digit(0)&middot;10<sup>0</sup> + digit(1)&middot;10<sup>1</sup> + &hellip;</code> — which <em>is</em> the number <code>N</code> itself. The weighted sum &equiv; <code>N (mod d)</code>.',
        'proof.s3': 'The running total is kept below <code>d</code> and stays congruent to <code>N</code>, so the final total is exactly the remainder of <code>N</code> divided by <code>d</code>. It is 0 exactly when <code>d</code> divides <code>N</code>. &#8718;',

