        'prior.r1c': 'The generalized rule: weights are the powers of ten reduced modulo <code>d</code>, and the weighted digit sum is congruent to <code>N</code>.',
        'prior.r1o': '<b>Complete overlap.</b> Pascal’s criterion <i>is</i> “replace each power of 10 by its remainder mod <code>D</code>”. Same weights, same rule.',
        'prior.r2c': 'The final total is the exact remainder, not merely a yes/no signal — presented above as a bonus.',
        'prior.r2o': '<b>Complete overlap.</b> The remainder-preserving property is Pascal’s original formulation, not something added on top of it.',
        'prior.r3c': 'The rule needs no coprimality condition and covers 2, 4, 8, 16, 20, 45 and every other divisor.',
        'prior.r3o': '<b>Complete overlap.</b> <code>a &equiv; &Sigma; (10 &minus; q)<sup>j</sup>&middot;a<sub>j</sub> (mod q)</code> holds unconditionally for every <code>q</code>, which is why no coprimality hypothesis was ever needed.',
        'prior.r4c': 'Every classical rule — 2, 3, 4, 8, 9, 11 — is a row of one table.',
        'prior.r4o': '<b>Complete overlap.</b> Their abstract says it outright: <i>“Well-known divisibility rules for exemplary divisors in the decimal system follow from the universal expression as special cases.”</i>',
        'prior.r5c': 'Weights of divisors built only from 2s and 5s collapse to 0; divisors coprime to 10 cycle; mixed divisors do both.',
        'prior.r5o': '<b>Complete overlap.</b> This is the textbook explanation of why the rules for 4 and 8 look only at the last digits.',
        'prior.r6c': 'The three-line proof.',
        'prior.r6o': '<b>Complete overlap.</b> The three steps are the usual induction on <code>10<sup>t</sup> &equiv; weight(t) (mod d)</code>.',
        'prior.r7c': 'The 1997 trimming equation <code>(x&middot;a1) + ((y&middot;a2)+1)&middot;b1</code>.',
        'prior.r7o': '<b>Same family.</b> Zbikowski’s test is <code>T<sub>q</sub>(a) = ā + &omega;<sub>q</sub>&middot;a<sub>0</sub></code> with <code>&omega;<sub>q</sub> = 10<sup>&minus;1</sup> mod q</code>, for divisors ending in 1, 3, 7, 9 — the 1997 equation with the classical multiplier.',
        'prior.r8c': '…and specifically as one member of a two-parameter family.',
        'prior.r8o': '<b>Exact identification, verified.</b> Their restricted rule <code>R = u&middot;B + w&middot;b</code> is valid when <code>N = 10w &minus; u</code> is a multiple of <code>d</code>. Substituting gives <code>10w &minus; x = y&middot;d</code>, so the 1997 equation is their rule at <code>q = y</code>. Checked for every <code>d</code> from 2 to 99: zero mismatches.',
        'prior.r9c': 'The corrected multiplier table <code>m</code> for divisors ending in 1, 3, 7, 9.',
        'prior.r9o': '<b>Complete overlap.</b> <code>m = 10<sup>&minus;1</sup> mod d</code> throughout — verified against <code>pow(10, -1, d)</code> for sixteen divisors. The <code>m = 5</code> row is the classical positive osculator, and the &times;5 walk for testing 7 is Kordemsky’s problem 320.',
        'prior.r10c': 'The trimming rule and the weighted-sum rule are the same rule run in opposite directions.',
        'prior.r10o': '<b>Complete overlap; it is the paper’s thesis.</b> Theorem 2: <code>(Stack &compfn; T<sub>q</sub>)<sup>n</sup>(a) = S<sub>q</sub>(a)</code>. Theorem 3: <code>(LStack &compfn; LT<sub>q</sub>)<sup>n</sup>(a) = B<sub>q</sub>(a)</code>. Trimming becomes summing, in both directions.',
        'prior.r11c': 'The trimming rule requires <code>gcd(d, 10) = 1</code>, which is why it cannot reach even divisors.',
        'prior.r11o': '<b>Complete overlap.</b> Wikipedia states it directly: the rule <i>“is really a rule for divisibility by any integer relatively prime to 10”</i>.',
        'prior.addsH3': 'What this project does add',
        'prior.addsIntro': 'With the above on the table, here is what appears to be genuinely original — stated small, because it is small.',
        'prior.add1': '<b>A closed form for the trimming coefficients, read off the divisor’s last digit alone.</b> <code>x = 10 + b2 &minus; b2&middot;&lceil;10/b2&rceil;</code> and <code>y = &lceil;10/b2&rceil; &minus; 1</code>. Zbikowski’s <code>&omega;<sub>q</sub></code> comes from a recipe and Grinberg &amp; Luryi’s <code>(u, w)</code> from a table; this is a formula in <code>b2</code> needing neither. Not found in print — but a convenience, not a theorem.',
        'prior.add2': '<b>The exactness criterion for that parametrization.</b> The identity <code>f(N) = ((y&middot;a2)+1)&middot;N &minus; (y&middot;a1)&middot;d</code>, the criterion <code>gcd((y&middot;a2)+1, d) = 1</code>, and the complete failure set below 100: 16, 18, 27, 36, 38, 45, 56, 57, 58, 76, 78, 87, 95, 96, 98. Verified two independent ways — by the criterion and by brute force — which agree exactly.',
        'prior.add3': '<b>An independent rediscovery in 1997, on paper, with a pocket calculator.</b> Not a contribution to mathematics. Still the reason this project exists.',
        'prior.add4': '<b>The engineering and the pedagogy.</b> A division-free implementation whose only operation on the divisor is subtraction, a full trace of every step, exhaustive verification over all 10,000 dividend/divisor pairs from 1 to 100, and this four-language visualization. Pascal’s criterion is in every number theory book; a runnable, traceable, tested presentation of it is rarer.',
